WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.455
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level two.

2
00:00:05.755 --> 00:00:09.129
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.429 --> 00:00:16.401
Unit ten: Real Estate Market. Today you will describe trends with noun phrases of change and prepositions.

4
00:00:16.701 --> 00:00:19.469
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.433 --> 00:00:24.165
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:25.017 --> 00:00:26.308
Property value.

7
00:00:27.317 --> 00:00:30.907
Property value means the amount of money a building or land is worth.

8
00:00:31.517 --> 00:00:34.306
The property value rose after the new road opened.

9
00:00:34.917 --> 00:00:37.720
Your turn. Say: Property value.

10
00:00:40.567 --> 00:00:41.719
Property value.

11
00:00:42.483 --> 00:00:43.705
Mortgage rate.

12
00:00:44.717 --> 00:00:47.993
Mortgage rate means the interest charged on a loan to buy a home.

13
00:00:48.583 --> 00:00:51.361
A lower mortgage rate helps first-time buyers.

14
00:00:51.950 --> 00:00:54.649
Your turn. Say: Mortgage rate.

15
00:00:57.500 --> 00:00:58.675
Mortgage rate.

16
00:00:59.417 --> 00:01:00.801
Housing supply.

17
00:01:01.817 --> 00:01:05.465
Housing supply means the number of homes available to buy or rent.

18
00:01:06.050 --> 00:01:08.665
Limited housing supply pushed prices up.

19
00:01:09.250 --> 00:01:11.937
Your turn. Say: Housing supply.

20
00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:16.405
Housing supply.

21
00:01:17.150 --> 00:01:18.662
Real estate agent.

22
00:01:19.650 --> 00:01:24.436
Real estate agent means a person who helps people buy, sell or rent property.

23
00:01:25.050 --> 00:01:27.804
A real estate agent showed us three flats.

24
00:01:28.417 --> 00:01:31.278
Your turn. Say: Real estate agent.

25
00:01:34.133 --> 00:01:35.808
Real estate agent.

26
00:01:36.550 --> 00:01:37.656
Down payment.

27
00:01:38.650 --> 00:01:42.194
Down payment means the first part of the price paid when buying a home.

28
00:01:42.783 --> 00:01:45.108
She saved for years for a down payment.

29
00:01:45.717 --> 00:01:48.543
Your turn. Say: Down payment.

30
00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:52.552
Down payment.

31
00:01:53.317 --> 00:01:54.492
Rental market.

32
00:01:55.483 --> 00:01:58.957
Rental market means the part of the economy where homes are rented.

33
00:01:59.550 --> 00:02:02.327
The rental market is busy near universities.

34
00:02:02.917 --> 00:02:05.790
Your turn. Say: Rental market.

35
00:02:08.633 --> 00:02:09.832
Rental market.

36
00:02:10.583 --> 00:02:11.782
Property tax.

37
00:02:12.783 --> 00:02:16.954
Property tax means a yearly tax paid by owners of land and buildings.

38
00:02:17.550 --> 00:02:20.084
Property tax pays for local services.

39
00:02:20.683 --> 00:02:23.603
Your turn. Say: Property tax.

40
00:02:26.467 --> 00:02:27.770
Property tax.

41
00:02:28.517 --> 00:02:29.634
Housing bubble.

42
00:02:30.650 --> 00:02:35.308
Housing bubble means a period when house prices rise unrealistically and then fall.

43
00:02:35.917 --> 00:02:38.462
The housing bubble burst in 2008.

44
00:02:39.050 --> 00:02:41.807
Your turn. Say: Housing bubble.

45
00:02:44.667 --> 00:02:45.946
Housing bubble.

46
00:02:46.733 --> 00:02:50.686
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:51.550 --> 00:02:58.324
Visal, a report says 'A two-percent rise in mortgage rates has led to a sharp fall in demand.' What kind of phrase is that?

48
00:02:59.417 --> 00:03:09.778
A noun phrase of change: a rise in, a fall in, an increase of, a drop of. They describe trends without long verbs.

49
00:03:10.883 --> 00:03:12.825
Which prepositions do I use?

50
00:03:13.917 --> 00:03:26.696
'In' follows the thing that changes: a rise in property value. 'Of' follows the amount: a rise of two percent. 'By' belongs to verbs: rose by two percent.

51
00:03:27.783 --> 00:03:29.992
How does that help real estate news?

52
00:03:31.083 --> 00:03:39.341
A shortage in housing supply, an increase in down payments and an expansion of the rental market can all be reported in one clear line.

53
00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:41.942
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:42.783 --> 00:03:47.825
Grammar focus: Noun phrases of change: a rise in and a rise of.

55
00:03:48.125 --> 00:03:49.312
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:50.017 --> 00:03:52.887
A sharp rise in mortgage rates cooled the market.

57
00:03:53.783 --> 00:03:56.688
An increase of five percent raised property values.

58
00:03:57.583 --> 00:04:00.279
A shortage in housing supply pushed rents up.

59
00:04:01.233 --> 00:04:08.297
Say a rise IN prices but a rise OF five percent. The verb forms use by: prices rose BY five percent.

60
00:04:09.050 --> 00:04:12.805
Be careful! Don't say: A rise by prices.

61
00:04:14.767 --> 00:04:17.056
Say: A rise in prices.

62
00:04:17.356 --> 00:04:18.938
Use 'in' after a rise.

63
00:04:19.733 --> 00:04:22.513
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:23.300 --> 00:04:24.870
Reading the Housing Market.

65
00:04:25.170 --> 00:04:27.808
Property news depends on a few key numbers.

66
00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:33.876
A sharp rise in mortgage rates makes a down payment harder to save for, so demand falls.

67
00:04:34.867 --> 00:04:40.280
A shortage in housing supply, however, can push property value up even when buyers are few.

68
00:04:41.267 --> 00:04:44.845
The rental market often grows when buying becomes too expensive.

69
00:04:45.833 --> 00:04:50.329
Experts warn that a housing bubble can burst when prices rise faster than incomes.

70
00:04:51.433 --> 00:04:55.598
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:56.450 --> 00:04:59.587
A sharp rise blank mortgage rates cooled the market.

72
00:04:59.887 --> 00:05:02.798
A, in. B, by. C, to of.

73
00:05:06.333 --> 00:05:07.961
The answer is A: in.

74
00:05:09.117 --> 00:05:14.019
A period when prices rise unrealistically, then fall, is a housing blank.

75
00:05:14.319 --> 00:05:17.531
A, bubble. B, supply. C, agent.

76
00:05:21.067 --> 00:05:23.043
The answer is A: bubble.

77
00:05:24.183 --> 00:05:26.717
The first part of the price paid is the blank payment.

78
00:05:27.017 --> 00:05:30.101
A, down. B, tax. C, rental.

79
00:05:33.667 --> 00:05:35.353
The answer is A: down.

80
00:05:36.517 --> 00:05:38.284
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:05:38.584 --> 00:05:44.571
A, A rise by 2%.. B, A rise of 2%.. C, A rise in of 2%..

82
00:05:48.133 --> 00:05:51.038
The answer is B: A rise of 2%..

83
00:05:52.183 --> 00:05:56.366
According to the reading, the rental market often grows when buying is too…

84
00:05:56.666 --> 00:06:00.017
A, expensive. B, cheap. C, easy.

85
00:06:03.567 --> 00:06:05.473
The answer is A: expensive.

86
00:06:06.667 --> 00:06:09.691
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:10.550 --> 00:06:14.337
How do rising mortgage rates affect whether people can afford to buy a home?

88
00:06:23.150 --> 00:06:24.836
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:25.136 --> 00:06:46.416
Rising mortgage rates increase the monthly cost of a loan, so a home that was affordable becomes too expensive. Buyers may need a larger down payment or may delay buying altogether. As demand falls, property values can stop rising. Meanwhile, more families rent, which strengthens the rental market.

90
00:06:46.716 --> 00:06:48.681
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:06:49.583 --> 00:06:55.904
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and noun phrases of change: a rise in and a rise of.

92
00:06:56.204 --> 00:07:01.991
Today's value is economic awareness. Understand how money and work affect your life choices.

93
00:07:02.291 --> 00:07:04.863
See you in Unit eleven. Goodbye!

94
00:07:05.163 --> 00:07:08.221
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

